## Why This Is Asked
Tests understanding of persistent data structures and O(1) updates without copying.

## Key Concepts
- Persistence via structural sharing
- Node-based linked representation
- Versioning by top pointer
- Space-time trade-offs and edge cases

## Code Example
```javascript
class Node { constructor(val, prev){ this.val = val; this.prev = prev; } }
class PersistentStack {
  constructor() { this.versions = [null]; } // version 0: empty
  push(version, val) { const top = this.versions[version]; const node = new Node(val, top); this.versions.push(node); return this.versions.length - 1; }
  pop(version) { const top = this.versions[version]; if (!top) return version; this.versions.push(top.prev); return this.versions.length - 1; }
  top(version) { const t = this.versions[version]; return t ? t.val : null; }
  isEmpty(version) { return this.versions[version] == null; }
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you manage memory for old versions (garbage collection)?
- How would you expose the API to external callers (version IDs, immutability guarantees)?
- Could you adapt this to a persistent queue or other structures with similar patterns?

## Why This Is Asked
Tests ability to implement a compact prefix-based dictionary with duplicates and fast prefix queries using a Trie.

## Key Concepts
- Trie with per-node counts; handling duplicates via passCount and endCount.
- Insert/erase adjust counts; erase stops if word not present.
- Prefix query is O(length(prefix)).

## Code Example
```javascript
class TrieNode{ constructor(){ this.next=new Array(26).fill(null); this.pass=0; this.end=0; }}
class Trie{ constructor(){ this.root=new TrieNode(); }
 insert(w){ let p=this.root; for(const ch of w){ const i=ch.charCodeAt(0)-97; if(!p.next[i]) p.next[i]=new TrieNode(); p=p.next[i]; p.pass++; } p.end++; }
 erase(w){ if(!this.contains(w)) return; let p=this.root; for(const ch of w){ const i=ch.charCodeAt(0)-97; p=p.next[i]; p.pass--; } p.end--; }
 countPrefix(pfx){ let p=this.root; for(const ch of pfx){ const i=ch.charCodeAt(0)-97; p=p.next[i]; if(!p) return 0; } return p.pass; }
 contains(w){ let p=this.root; for(const ch of w){ const i=ch.charCodeAt(0)-97; p=p.next[i]; if(!p) return false; } return p.end>0; }
 }
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you adapt for Unicode inputs or dynamic alphabets?
- What are the memory implications for large vocabularies with many prefixes?

